If z is extensional in its first argument and lies in every iterator functionality, then for every f and x there is some n such that the double application of z to f and x equals the n-fold iterate of f at x.

Extensionality replaces f by the graph of its application. On the iterate equivalence generated by that graph at x, the graph acts by advancing the iterate count by one, so it lies in the corresponding function space. Functionality of z and the closure theorem place the application of z in that function space and then place its value at x, the zeroth iterate, in the equivalence. The witness is a count n together with equality to the iterate of the graph, and the graph law for the iterator rewrites that iterate as the iterate of the original f.
